    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d156">

                <pb facs="0285" n="195" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg195"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01 Manchuria/1102: Telegram (part air)</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gilbert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-05-25T14:00:00+01:00">May 25, 1934—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 26—8:55 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>95. Japanese Consul General here informs me that he has received official
                    notification from his Government of Salvador’s recognition of “Manchukuo”. He
                    states that he understands that thus far this is the only act of any Government
                    which Tokyo construes as constituting formal recognition (Consulate’s 858
                    Political, March 28,<anchor n="27" xml:id="d156-f1"/> page 5).</p>
                <p>It may be noted incidentally that Salvador was not represented in the
                    extraordinary Assembly which on February 24, 1933, voted on the nonrecognition
                    recommendations.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gilbert</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
